// The machine: cars, queues, dwell, a programmable schedule
// (SYSTEMS.md §1.3) — and, when nobody is calling it, the inserter.
//
// **This is the only built shaft now** (SYSTEMS.md §6.18). There used to
// be a dumbwaiter beneath it, item-only and autonomous, and
// examples/lift.rs measured that as the *better* of the two at eight
// floors — and better still the wider the hull got, because nothing
// rides a dumbwaiter so nobody walks to one. Two rungs where the lower
// one wins on both counts is not a ladder. So the lift does that job in
// the gaps between its riders, and the price below is the one rung's.
#![enable(implicit_some)]
ShaftDef(
    id: "shaft.elevator",
    name: "Elevator",
    short: "ELV",
    kind: Elevator,
    // **Two poles after the logistics expansion; rope remains the real
    // gate.** The chain-first whole-run fixture reaches the Refugia with
    // 47 rope but only one pole after spending 110 poles keeping the
    // walking house repaired. At ten poles the lift vanished from the
    // 31–36 minute run; at two it returns without bypassing the fiber →
    // rope chain or the permanent full-height slot cost. This is a
    // measured affordability correction, not a playtested final price.
    build_cost: [
        // **Rope, from M5 — and deliberately not mechanisms.**
        //
        // The spec (`SYSTEMS.md` §5.3) gated this behind mechanisms, and
        // therefore behind alloy, scrap and the ruin belt, on the
        // reasoning that route choice should reach into the transport
        // layer. The reasoning is right and the material was wrong,
        // which building it made obvious: a tower needs a salvage rig, a
        // forge and a fitter before it can put in an elevator, which is
        // four more rooms and two more floors than a five-floor tower
        // has, and every one of those floors is more lamps drawing on
        // the same burner. Measured on the golden recorder:
        // every buffer in the tower empty, the cutter arm at 0/8, and a
        // fixture that could not be recorded at all.
        //
        // **Rope makes the same point better.** Fiber comes from the
        // *middle* bands — neither deep shade nor open sun — so the gate
        // still says "your route decides whether you can build this",
        // and what it now says is "you have to have walked ordinary
        // ground", which is a more interesting sentence than "you have
        // to have gone to the ruins". Mechanisms keep the seed scatterer,
        // which is a fair consumer for a tier-two item and does not have
        // to exist by minute twenty.
        // **Four, not six, because rope is the real gate and six of it
        // was two gates.** Rope needs a fiber comb
        // feeding a ropery, and a ropery makes one coil from two fiber
        // every four seconds — so six coils is a second wait stacked on
        // top of the chain that produces them. The argument below is
        // about *which material*, and it is unchanged: fiber comes from
        // the middle bands, so the gate still says "you have to have
        // walked ordinary ground".
        // **Two, not three, because rope arrives in ones.** A ropery
        // makes one coil from two fiber every four seconds, so each
        // coil is a separate wait behind a separate pair of fiber, and
        // the last one is the whole difference between a lift that
        // arrives mid-run and one that arrives at the end of it.
        //
        // The gate is *which material*, not how many coils — fiber
        // comes from the middle bands, so it still says "you have to
        // have walked ordinary ground" (SYSTEMS.md §5.10). Three said
        // that no better than two and said it later.
        CostEntryDef(item: "item.rope", amount: 2),
    ],
    // Frame and guide rail per crossed deck. A two-floor lift retains
    // the old one-pole entry price; height now has an honest material
    // cost instead of being free once the machinery exists.
    span_cost: [CostEntryDef(item: "item.poles", amount: 1)],
    min_span: 2,
    // Unlimited — the elevator is chosen floors, not a fixed hop.
    max_span: 0,
    // In units: a crew member is 1, a carried load adds another. 4
    // seats roughly two laden crew, the "small crowd" scale the
    // elevator earns its slot column for.
    capacity: 4,
    // Nearly 4x the stairs' pace per floor.
    ticks_per_floor: 8,
    // **Four, between the elevator's five and the dumbwaiter's one.**
    // One shaft doing both jobs runs nearly all the time — it fetches
    // stock in every gap between riders — so the per-floor draw is paid
    // far more often than the elevator's ever was. Dropping it is what
    // stops the merge quietly doubling the tower's lift bill.
    charge_per_floor: 4,
    cars: 1,
    // **Three, and the third is for a tower nobody has built yet.**
    //
    // A shaft is a *column*, and a column is the scarcest thing a tower
    // owns — it costs a slot on every floor it passes through, for
    // ever. So the late answer to a queue should not have to be another
    // column: `AddCar` puts a second and a third car in the shaft that
    // is already there, and costs no width at all.
    //
    // Capacity is per car (`service_stop` boards up to `capacity` for
    // each), so a second car is a second carload rather than a faster
    // one — which is the right shape for the problem it solves. A queue
    // is people waiting for a *turn*, not for speed.
    max_cars: 3,
    // Two thirds of the shaft itself, in the same two materials. Dear
    // enough to be a decision and cheap enough to beat digging a second
    // column, which is the comparison it exists to win.
    car_cost: [
        CostEntryDef(item: "item.poles", amount: 6),
        CostEntryDef(item: "item.rope", amount: 2),
        CostEntryDef(item: "item.mechanisms", amount: 1),
    ],
    // Items moved per autonomous trip, inherited from the dumbwaiter
    // unchanged: a little more than one crew member's `carry_capacity`
    // of 3, so the lift beats a hauler on volume without making the
    // trip pointless.
    batch: 4,
    // Useful machine trunk, but weaker than a dedicated riser.
    power_capacity: 6,
)
